Introduction

This essay is based on and aims to critically reflect and analyze my experience as a teacher under observational experience and knowledge in the CLC classroom and how I could change the ways I normalized in the classroom. We have to focus the article and have clear examples, discussions supporting what we have connected and then shown. We will see different types of norms and grueling helps and try to see how I made thechange in the process, what is still lacking in the process and how future changes could help me be a successful teacher and skill development? The task is to find out what methods are needed to expand the CLC environment.

Contextual Education I saw a lot of children in my class, they have heard a lot of stories in my class and have learned so many things, some were very entertaining and the children loved the class and joined the class. Sometimes I made them laugh, sometimes I made them sad, and sometimes I made them immersed. Provide some of the examples you have observed in children, how to teach them, what to teach and then what changes need to be learned. The cultural learning experience of students is judged by their ability to adapt to classroom classes and inter-cultural fields. For contextual education, we can connect it with the learning strategies that one can use for teaching and learning in the classroom. The previous experience studying in the classroom for making it attractive for children. Teaching them different norms, traditions, and new class learning content both in terms of processes and product. It may not be easy for foreign teachers to offload love and learn to the CLC classroom. The learning pressure is the success that a foreign teacher can make to accumulate in the lives of the students he teaches.

The CLC classroom is a classroom specially designed for foreign children who are attending kindergarten or lower primary. It is our own (foreign teachers') little space in the context of local schools or culture. Although language teachers teach content in English as part of the curriculum and teaching seamlessly, most foreign children are learning Chinese every day because their classmates are Chinese. Hogeveenz (2002), a well-known teacher educator, discusses it: "Native speakers and language teachers should not be allowed to teach without taking into account the cultural differences between their students and their own students' home country, but must take into account how the students' culture of their parents may be the same or naturally different from their own culture. May influence their lives and their parents of their parents 'culture. With this idea in mind, theé’ˆå¯¹Cultural Learning Curriculum or cultural curriculum content is designed to ensure that the foreign children are not only in the CLC classroom. Knowing the knowledge of China but also to accept the knowledge of China.

In my previous work experience, I came to know about Chinese culture for teacher's training cultural education. The teacher's training course was based on a rapid survey of Chinese philosophy and Chinese literature. When I found my best knowledge of teaching pictures and conversations, I tried to teach the students a lesson based on Chinese ancient myths and related myths. I think of their interest in a more scientific and useful way. Later, the teacher who taught me this test liked my lesson, so she invited me to teach in first grade when I was in teacher training course. The first tests were mainly based on the knowledge of Chinese. Four areas of study include folk history, geography, Chinese and marketing. Four-year-old students do not have the ability to study new knowledge, so we teach them with stories, songs, group games, role play and other teaching methods. Four-year-old students celebrate Chinese festivals, sing Chinese songs and know the stories of Chinese ancient myths. By doing all this, the students are not only learning Chinese knowledge, they are also accepting Chinese culture and changing the way they think in their mother tongue. The discussed curriculum is just a small part of the big mainstream education system in China. The Chinese education system is based on rote learning and the students memorize concepts from texts, primary school textbooks and exams. The teachers teach in the same way. Chinese students are not taught to question and are not encouraged to question. Students are not taught by teachers to because of the strict educational system. They should follow instructions and be quiet when I speak. Chinese students are not used to learning in a creative, engaging and interactive way because the Chinese system does not trust their students' independence. So, how to define this? Where and where is the difference between it?

According to the knowledge building theory, the audience (students, teachers, administrators and parents) who participate in the interaction through written word or word can construct their own knowledge. The explanation of the theory is based on the observation of the situation and attitude of the audience who speaks or listens in the specific situation. As one of the most important, 2023-02-26 09:44:44, 2023-02-26 09:44:44)) {"userId":1,"exception":"[object] (Illuminate\\Database\\QueryException(code: 22001): SQLSTATE[22001]: String data, right truncated: 1406 Data too long for column 'text' at row 1 (Connection: mysql, SQL: insert into `text_books` (`title`, `text`, `updated_at`, `created_at`) values (Want to join us at I^3 and learn more about, and to contribute to, cutting edge interoperability developments?

